CM_ BU_ HOST "PC application (PROJ_VESC-CAN-Flash / mic.py MIC.retrieve_info) that polls a VESC controller for its firmware/hardware/UUID info.";
CM_ BU_ VESC "A VESC/MIC motor controller answering an info poll over CAN.";

CM_ BO_ 2147485706 "COMM_FW_VERSION poll sent to controller ID 10 (arbitration ID = 0x800 + 10 = 0x80A, CAN_PACKET_PROCESS_SHORT_BUFFER). Field layout confirmed against pybldc's own PROCESS_SHORT_BUFFER encoding (pybldc.py _send_implementation, len<=6 branch: [self._id, 0, *data]) -- mic.py sends 7 zero bytes, i.e. SenderId=0 (host identifies itself as controller 0), ProcessAndRespond=0 ('process at receiver and send a response'), CommandId=0 (VESC comm protocol COMM_FW_VERSION), Reserved padding=0. Controller replies with a VESC_INFO_RESPONSE_CHUNK/_END sequence.";
CM_ BO_ 2147485790 "Same as VESC_INFO_REQUEST_ID10, addressed to controller ID 94 (0x800 + 94 = 0x85E).";
CM_ BO_ 2147485724 "Same as VESC_INFO_REQUEST_ID10, addressed to controller ID 28 (0x800 + 28 = 0x81C).";

CM_ BO_ 2147484928 "One CAN_PACKET_FILL_RX_BUFFER chunk of the COMM_FW_VERSION reply (arbitration ID fixed at 0x500 for every controller -- the ID encodes the destination address (host = 0), not the responding controller's own address, so it does NOT by itself identify which controller ID (10/94/28) sent it; the host only knows because it has exactly one poll outstanding at a time). Format is [FragmentOffset, Data0..Data6] -- confirmed against pybldc's CAN_PACKET_FILL_RX_BUFFER send logic (pybldc.py _send_implementation, 'Fill 7 bytes at a time': send_buffer=[i, *data[i:i+7]]). FragmentOffset is the byte offset, within the reassembled reply buffer, where this frame's 7 Data bytes begin; successive chunks carry FragmentOffset 0, 7, 14, 21, 28, 35, 42. mic.py reconstructs the reply by concatenating raw 8-byte frames (offset byte included) until VESC_INFO_RESPONSE_END, then slices fixed indices -- which is equivalent to, and here re-expressed as, per-chunk fields: FragmentOffset=0: Data0=CommandId echo (0x00), Data1=FW major version, Data2=FW minor version, Data3-Data6=HW name chars[0:4]. FragmentOffset=7: Data0-Data3=HW name chars[4:8], Data4=not decoded by mic.py (likely HW-name terminator/reserved), Data5-Data6=UUID bytes[0:2]. FragmentOffset=14: Data0-Data6=UUID bytes[2:9] (7 bytes). FragmentOffset=21: Data0-Data2=UUID bytes[9:12] (completes the 12-byte STM32 unique-chip ID -- this UUID IS what this tool/VESC ecosystem uses as the board's de-facto serial number; there is no separate SN field), Data3=not decoded by mic.py, Data4=FW status (0=STABLE, 1=BETA, 2=ALPHA), Data5-Data6=not decoded by mic.py. FragmentOffset=28/35/42: entirely outside what mic.py parses (real VESC firmware likely carries HW_TYPE, test-version number, pairing/config flags here per commands.c, unconfirmed in this codebase).";
CM_ BO_ 2147485440 "End-of-transfer marker for a VESC_INFO_RESPONSE_CHUNK sequence (arbitration ID fixed at 0x700 for every controller, same addressing caveat as VESC_INFO_RESPONSE_CHUNK; this is CAN_PACKET_PROCESS_RX_BUFFER, ID = 7). mic.py only uses receipt of this ID as an 'end of buffer' signal and never reads its payload, so this 6-byte layout ([DestId, ProcessAndRespond, TotalLengthHi/Lo, Crc16Hi/Lo]) is INFERRED by symmetry with pybldc's own outbound PROCESS_RX_BUFFER encoding (pybldc.py _send_implementation, >6-byte branch: [self._id, 0, *pack_uint16(len(data)), *pack_uint16(crc16_ccitt(data))]), not empirically verified against a bus capture -- confirm with a real trace (e.g. candump/SavvyCAN) before relying on it.";
